nmap <A-j>      move-down         "Down a semitone"
nmap <A-S-k>    move-octave-up    "Up an octave"
nmap <A-S-j>    move-octave-down  "Down an octave"
nmap =          note-lengthen     "Lengthen to the grid"
nmap -          note-shorten      "Shorten to the grid"
nmap +          note-lengthen-fine "Lengthen by a nudge"
nmap _          note-shorten-fine "Shorten by a nudge"

" Editing fields: Enter edits the field under the cursor, typing a value or a change such as
" +10 or +1/16 (Enter sets it, Escape leaves it); + and - (or Ctrl-A and Ctrl-X) step it, with
" counts; in the piano roll, Ctrl-A and Ctrl-X step the current note's velocity.
" The inspector: e edits the fields of the note under the cursor in the piano roll, or the
" section on the section strip, starting on the one it was last on; in the event list it
" edits the field under the cursor in place, as Enter does. Tab and Shift-Tab set the field
" and go to the next or the one before, round from the last to the first; Enter sets it and
" leaves, Escape leaves it; Up and Down step it.
keysection "Editing fields"
nmap <CR>       field-edit        "Edit this field"
nmap <CR>       bar-open          "Open the bar in the piano roll"
nmap <CR>       section-play      "Play from this section"
nmap +          field-increase-fine "Step up"
nmap =          field-increase    "Step up"
nmap <C-a>      number-increase   "Step up"
nmap -          field-decrease    "Step down"
nmap _          field-decrease-fine "Step down"
nmap <C-x>      number-decrease   "Step down"
nmap <C-a>      velocity-up       "Velocity up"
nmap <C-x>      velocity-down     "Velocity down"
nmap e          inspector-edit    "Edit in the inspector"

" Deleting, yanking and putting: d and y take a range, up to a boundary its key names (d]b up
" to the next bar line, d]s the next section, dG the end, dj this event and the next), or an
" object: ib this bar's events, ab this bar as time, which d takes out, closing the gap up to
" the section's end, in the note or event under the cursor, ic its chord, is this section's
" events, and it the track's events. An object never reaches beyond the cursor's place: on the
" section strip, is is the section's events on every track, as the section itself, which d
" takes out of the song, and ie the whole song. dd and yy are this bar's events; x deletes the
" note or event under the cursor and Y yanks it. p lays what was yanked or deleted over what's
" there, and P puts it in, moving the rest of the section later; bars go to the start of the
" cursor's bar. Alt-p and Alt-P put the same at the cursor's pitch too, in the piano roll, the
" notes keeping their intervals to the pitch the cursor was on when they were taken (or to
" their lowest). "a before a yank, delete or put uses register a ("A adds to it).
keysection "Deleting, yanking and putting"
nmap d          operator-delete   "Delete"
nmap y          operator-yank     "Yank"
nmap x          event-delete      "Delete this event"
nmap Y          event-yank        "Yank this"
nmap p          put-merge         "Put over what's there"
nmap p          put-after         "Put after this section"
nmap P          put-insert        "Put in, moving the rest later"
nmap P          put-before        "Put before this section"
nmap <A-p>      put-merge-at-pitch "Put at the cursor's pitch, over what's there"
nmap <A-S-p>    put-insert-at-pitch "Put in at the cursor's pitch, moving the rest later"
vmap p          put-replace       "Put in place of the selection"
vmap P          put-replace       "Put in place of the selection"
nmap "          register-use      "Register"

" The other operators take the same ranges and objects, a count before them saying how far:
" > and < transpose notes a semitone (3>ib three), t and T move events later and earlier by
" the grid step (in the piano roll, its notes), q quantises notes' starts to the grid, asking
" whether the pedals go with them, ^ sets or changes velocity, asking how (80, +10, 80%), ~
" humanises notes, moving each a little at random in time and velocity (humanizetime and
" humanizevel say how far), and ! transforms them, the transform's key following.
" Doubled (>>, qq), they take this bar. To them ab is ib and as is is, since they change
" events in place.
keysection "Operators"
nmap >          operator-transpose-up "Transpose up"
nmap <lt>       operator-transpose-down "Transpose down"
nmap t          operator-shift-later "Shift later by a grid step"
nmap T          operator-shift-earlier "Shift earlier by a grid step"
nmap q          operator-quantize "Quantise"
nmap ^          operator-velocity "Velocity"
nmap ~          operator-humanize "Humanise"
nmap !          operator-transform "Transform"

" Undo, repeating and macros: u and Ctrl-R undo and redo, and . repeats the last change. Qa
